course: dictionary
lesson: 7495
title: Postcards
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A postcard is a card, often with a picture on one side, that you write a short message on and send by post. It has no envelope, so the message is easy to read. Look at the words around the word to find clues: «Léa's postcard» tells you someone wrote a card and sent it.
explain: «Ellie and her mum make a memory box for Grandad: his glasses, a photo, the wrapper from his favourite sweets and a postcard.» The other things in the box are small keepsakes, so a postcard is a small card that people keep and treasure.
explain: «So when you look at a star you see old light, like getting a postcard posted long ago.» A postcard posted long ago carries a message from the past, which helps you picture light that left a star long ago and only arrives now.
explain: To check your meaning, swap it into the sentence. Put "a card with a short message" in place of postcard: "Léa's card - Je suis à Nice avec ma famille" still makes sense, so you have the right meaning.
fact: A postcard is a card you send by post, often with a picture on one side.
fact: You write a short message on a postcard, and it does not need an envelope.
fact: A postcard can be any small picture card, not only a holiday card.
fact: In maths, postcards can be counted and priced: 3 postcards at 50p each cost £1.50.
fact: «Postcard» is a noun: one postcard, two postcards.
exercise: «Ellie and her mum make a memory box for Grandad: his glasses, a photo, the wrapper from his favourite sweets and a postcard.» What is a postcard in this box? | *A small card you keep and treasure | A pair of shoes | A big heavy box | A type of sweet
exercise: In maths, 3 postcards cost 50p each. How much do they cost altogether? | £15 | £3.50 | *£1.50 | 50p
exercise: Which word is the plural of postcard? | postcardes | *postcards | postcardy | postcard's
test: What does a postcard usually NOT need? | A short message | A picture on one side | An address | *An envelope
test: In «So when you look at a star you see old light, like getting a postcard posted long ago», what is the postcard compared to? | *Old light arriving from the past | A new phone | A school bell | A cold wind
exam: «Léa's postcard - Je suis à Nice avec ma famille.» What is a postcard here? | A ticket for a train | A shop that sells cards | *A card with a short message, sent by post | A map of the city of Nice
